[
  {
    "id": "P0001",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Found findings. The most consequential are corruption of existing entries during append/merge, lossy raw copying, and unreadable results from `finish_into_readable()`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0002",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "All behavior findings below were reproduced with local regression tests against unchanged source. Test names refer to [review_regressions.rs](/work/tmp/review/tests/review_regressions.rs), unless otherwise specified.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0003",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Append and merge lose required ZIP64 offsets \u2014 [src/write.rs:2094](/work/crate/src/write.rs:2094). Existing entries should remain readable. Parsing removes ZIP64 extra fields, but central-directory writing does not regenerate them. A five-byte entry at offset `0x100000000` reads correctly initially; after append or merge, reading fails with `Invalid local file header`. Appending in place can therefore make existing contents inaccessible. Evidence: `append_preserves_zip64_header_offset`, `merge_generates_zip64_header_offset`; both used sparse files.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0004",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Append and merge discard Implode decoding flags \u2014 [src/types.rs:864](/work/crate/src/types.rs:864). Rewriting metadata should preserve flags needed to decode unchanged contents. `flags()` reconstructs only encryption, UTF-8 and descriptor bits, dropping Implode\u2019s dictionary/tree flags. The existing `legacy/implode.zip` fixture reads successfully before rewriting; both a no-op append and a merge produce `too many codeword lengths`. Evidence: `append_preserves_implode_flags`, `merge_preserves_implode_flags`, with `legacy-zip` enabled.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0005",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Raw copying removes the ZipCrypto encrypted flag \u2014 [src/read.rs:1832](/work/crate/src/read.rs:1832). A raw copy should preserve the interpretation of its unchanged bytes. `options()` preserves AES metadata but loses ZipCrypto encryption state. Copying an untouched `by_index_raw()` entry succeeds, but the result reports `encrypted() == false` and fails with `Invalid checksum`. Evidence: `raw_copy_zipcrypto`, `raw_copy_zipcrypto_payload`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0006",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Raw copying a freshly opened decrypted AES entry silently produces an unreadable copy \u2014 [src/write.rs:1336](/work/crate/src/write.rs:1336). The API should copy the complete entry or reject a reader whose required bytes have already been consumed. Password validation consumes the AES prefix before the caller reads anything; `take_raw_reader()` cannot recover it, and the copied byte count is not checked. Copying a fresh `by_name_decrypt()` result returns success, but reopening the destination returns `InvalidPassword`. Evidence: `raw_copy_decrypted_aes_file`. Existing copying through `by_index_raw()` passes.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0007",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Raw copying loses symlink type and extra fields \u2014 [src/write.rs:1316](/work/crate/src/write.rs:1316). The raw-copy documentation promises copied metadata. Reconstructing entries through `SimpleFileOptions` strips file-type bits and extra fields. A copied symlink becomes a regular file containing its target text; a custom `0xbeef` field disappears. Callers copying directory trees or metadata-bearing archives lose their original meaning. Evidence: `raw_copy_symlink`, `raw_copy_metadata`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0008",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "`finish_into_readable()` exposes ZIP64 sentinels as actual sizes \u2014 [src/write.rs:2078](/work/crate/src/write.rs:2078). The returned archive should behave like reopening `finish()` output. Finalization replaces the in-memory sizes with `4294967295`, then hands that metadata directly to the reader. A five-byte Stored entry written with `large_file(true)` reports both sizes incorrectly and fails with `Invalid checksum` when read to completion. Evidence: `zip64_finish_into_readable`, `zip64_finish_into_readable_payload`; `zip64_finish_reopen_control` passes.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0009",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "`finish_into_readable()` cannot read AES entries \u2014 [src/write.rs:785](/work/crate/src/write.rs:785). The returned archive should support the same reads as reparsed output. Writer metadata retains `CompressionMethod::Aes`, whereas normal parsing substitutes the underlying compression method. Finalization succeeds, but `by_name_decrypt()` returns `Compression method not supported`. Evidence: `aes_finish_into_readable`; `aes_finish_reopen_control` passes.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0010",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Valid Unicode comments can prevent opening an entire archive \u2014 [src/read.rs:1530](/work/crate/src/read.rs:1530). The Unicode-comment checksum must use the original comment bytes. The implementation checks the already-decoded UTF-8 string instead. An archive with CP437 `caf\\x82` and a correctly checksummed Unicode comment `caf\u00e9` is rejected as `Could not find EOCD`. Evidence: `unicode_comment_with_cp437_original`; Python\u2019s `zipfile` successfully reads the fixture and reports no CRC failure.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0011",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "An overlong filename aborts the process instead of returning an error \u2014 [src/types.rs:902](/work/crate/src/types.rs:902). A filename exceeding the ZIP format\u2019s `u16` byte limit should produce a `ZipError`. A 65,536-byte name panics during conversion; unwinding then triggers another panic in central-directory writing at [src/types.rs:934](/work/crate/src/types.rs:934). Ordinary writer cleanup therefore aborts the process. Evidence: `too_long_filename_returns_error`; the standalone [review_long_name example](/work/tmp/review/examples/review_long_name.rs) exited **134** with \u201cpanic in a destructor during cleanup.\u201d\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0012",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Stream visitors silently receive no central-directory metadata \u2014 [src/read/stream.rs:48](/work/crate/src/read/stream.rs:48). `visit()` promises file contents and subsequent metadata. `read_zipfile_from_stream()` consumes 30 bytes of the first central header before returning `None`; metadata parsing starts at the wrong position, and its error is discarded. A two-entry archive returns success with two file callbacks and zero metadata callbacks. Comments and permission restoration are consequently lost. Evidence: `stream_metadata_count`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0013",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "SeekFrom::End on a stored entry uses the end of the entire archive. src/read.rs:284. A ZipFileSeek returned by by_name_seek for a Stored entry forwards SeekFrom::End to the archive reader without translating it to the entry extent. For an archive containing multiple files, seeking to End(-2) on the first file therefore selects bytes near the archive footer rather than that file's last two bytes. Applications that read entry tails through the seekable API receive unrelated archive data.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0014",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Extraction never restores directory permissions \u2014 [src/read.rs:898](/work/crate/src/read.rs:898). Directories made writable during extraction should receive their archived permissions afterward, consistent with the implementation\u2019s deferred-permission design. Directory entries `continue` before being added to that deferred list. An archived directory with mode `0500` extracts successfully as `0755`. Evidence: `extract_directory_permissions`, on Linux.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0015",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Seekable entry reads return encrypted bytes as file contents \u2014 [src/read.rs:1094](/work/crate/src/read.rs:1094). A normal entry-reading API should return decoded contents or an unsupported/password-required error. `by_index_seek()` checks compression but ignores encryption. For a Stored ZipCrypto entry containing five plaintext bytes, `by_name_seek()` returns 17 encrypted bytes, while `by_name()` correctly requires a password. Evidence: `seek_api_encrypted_entry`. This finding concerns incorrect returned data.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0016",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Directory lookup through the Path API fails \u2014 [src/read.rs:1068](/work/crate/src/read.rs:1068). An entry created using `add_directory_from_path(\"dir/subdir/\")` should be retrievable using that same path. Normalization removes the trailing slash, so `by_path()` searches for a different name and returns `FileNotFound`; `by_name(\"dir/subdir/\")` succeeds. Evidence: `directory_by_path`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0017",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Streaming `data_start()` panics despite documenting zero \u2014 [src/read.rs:1818](/work/crate/src/read.rs:1818). The streaming-reader documentation explicitly specifies `data_start == 0`. The associated `OnceLock` remains empty, and the accessor unconditionally unwraps it. Reading this documented metadata field therefore panics on an ordinary streamed entry. Evidence: `stream_data_start`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0018",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Stream writers omit required descriptors for raw copies and directories \u2014 [src/write.rs:1088](/work/crate/src/write.rs:1088), [src/write.rs:1451](/work/crate/src/write.rs:1451). Entries marked with general-purpose bit 3 require data descriptors. Both paths set that flag but bypass descriptor writing. Evidence: `stream_raw_copy`, `stream_directory_descriptor`; `stream_regular_file_descriptor_control` passes. The raw-copy result remains readable through this crate\u2019s central-directory reader; the confirmed defect is inconsistent ZIP structure, with interoperability consequences for readers relying on descriptors.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0019",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Access-only timestamps are misreported as modification times \u2014 [src/extra_fields/extended_timestamp.rs:51](/work/crate/src/extra_fields/extended_timestamp.rs:51). A valid local field containing only an access timestamp should expose it through `ac_time()`. For flags `0x02`, length 5 and timestamp 42, the parser instead returns `mod_time() == Some(42)` and `ac_time() == None`. Callers receive the wrong timestamp category. Evidence: `access_only_extended_timestamp`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0020",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "The documented Bzip2 default is incorrect \u2014 [src/write.rs:430](/work/crate/src/write.rs:430), [src/compression.rs:234](/work/crate/src/compression.rs:234). Documentation promises Bzip2 when enabled without Deflate. Actual defaults use Stored whenever Deflate is absent, so callers relying on the documented default receive uncompressed output. Evidence: [review_bzip2_default.rs](/work/tmp/review/tests/review_bzip2_default.rs), executed with `--no-default-features --features bzip2`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0021",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Strict Clippy fails on the supplied toolchain \u2014 [src/write.rs:2173](/work/crate/src/write.rs:2173). Rust/Clippy **1.99.1** rejects the nested condition with `clippy::collapsible_match` under `-D warnings`, for both all-features and no-default-features configurations. This blocks the project\u2019s strict lint command. Allowing only that lint makes the all-features/all-targets check pass.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0022",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Documentation contains broken links and fails strict rustdoc \u2014 [src/read.rs:1617](/work/crate/src/read.rs:1617), [src/spec.rs:95](/work/crate/src/spec.rs:95), [src/spec.rs:143](/work/crate/src/spec.rs:143). All-features documentation has four unresolved links. No-default-features adds unresolved `BufWriter` links at [src/write.rs:660](/work/crate/src/write.rs:660) and line 661. Normal all-features documentation builds with warnings; `RUSTDOCFLAGS='-D warnings'` makes both configurations fail.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0023",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Validation used Rust/Cargo **1.99.1**, on `aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu`, with `RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0`. Commands ran from `/work/crate` unless stated otherwise.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0024",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "| Command | Outcome |\n|---|---|\n| `cargo test --locked --all-features` | 178 unit/integration tests and 9 doctests passed. The known 4 GiB doctest was killed by SIGKILL; command exited 101. |\n| `cargo test --locked --no-default-features` | 106 unit/integration tests and 9 doctests passed; same memory-blocked doctest. |\n| `cargo test --locked -- --skip spec::ZIP64_BYTES_THR` | 174 passed; one filtered out. |\n| `cargo test --locked --no-default-features --features deflate-flate2-zlib-rs -- --skip spec::ZIP64_BYTES_THR` | 127 passed; one filtered out. |\n| `cargo test --locked --no-default-features --features deflate-zopfli -- --skip spec::ZIP64_BYTES_THR` | 116 passed; one filtered out. |\n| `cargo test --locked --all-features --doc -- --skip spec::ZIP64_BYTES_THR` | 9 passed; one filtered out. |\n| `cargo check --locked --all-features --all-targets` | Passed. |\n| `cargo fmt --all -- --check` | Passed. |\n| `cargo clippy --locked --all-features --all-targets -- -D warnings` | Failed as finding 18. The equivalent no-default-features command also failed. |\n| Same all-features Clippy command with `-A clippy::collapsible_match` appended | Passed. |\n| `cargo doc --locked --no-deps --all-features` | Passed with four warnings. |\n| `RUSTDOCFLAGS='-D warnings' cargo doc --locked --no-deps --all-features` | Failed with four broken links; no-default-features failed with six. |\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0025",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Scratch verification ran from `/work/tmp/review`:\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0026",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "- `cargo test --locked --all-features --test review_regressions -- --nocapture`: **22 failed assertions and 5 passing controls/documented-behavior checks**.\n- `cargo test --locked --no-default-features --features bzip2 --test review_bzip2_default`: one failed assertion.\n- `cargo run --locked --all-features --example review_long_name`: exit 134.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0027",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Only the scratch copy received two test files, one generated ZIP fixture and one example. `diff -qr /work/crate/src /work/tmp/review/src` confirmed identical source trees. Logs remain under `/work/tmp/`. No production fixes were made.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0028",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "findings. Coverage emphasized read/write round trips, metadata preservation, streaming and feature configurations. The memory-heavy doctest remains environment-blocked; its SIGKILL is not treated as a defect. MSRV 1.83, Miri, docs.rs configuration and other platforms were not checked. No dedicated security assessment was performed. Last clock reading: **769.5 seconds \u2014 12.8206 of 45 minutes used**."
  },
  {
    "id": "P0029",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "The review found findings: 10 High, 9 Medium, and 5 Low**, using [tooling]\u2019s severity definitions. All 178 existing unit and integration tests passed, but focused regressions demonstrated incorrect output, unexpected panics, and metadata loss. The full test command also encountered the documented memory-heavy doctest failure.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0030",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "No production source was edited. Harnesses, generated fixtures, and logs are under `/work/tmp`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0031",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "High\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0032",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "ZIP field-length conversions can abort the process\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0033",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "`crate/src/types.rs:902`, `crate/src/types.rs:938`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0034",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "Expected: unrepresentable field lengths return an error.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0035",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "Observed: a 65,536-byte filename panics at `try_into().unwrap()`. Appending an otherwise valid archive containing a 40,000-byte CP437 comment also panics: decoding expands its UTF-8 representation beyond 65,535 bytes. `finish` then panics again during `Drop`, aborting the process.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0036",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "Evidence: executed `long_filename_returns_error_without_panic` and the separate `append-long-comment` binary.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0037",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "validate encoded byte lengths before modifying writer state and use fallible conversions.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0038",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "`finish_into_readable` exposes ZIP64 sentinel sizes as actual sizes\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0039",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "`crate/src/write.rs:2078`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0040",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "Expected: the returned archive behaves like reopening the written bytes.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0041",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "Observed: writing five bytes with `large_file(true)` produces a returned entry reporting 4,294,967,295 bytes. Reading it consumes central-directory bytes and fails its checksum. Reopening the same bytes correctly reports five bytes.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0042",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "Consequence: callers receive incorrect metadata and cannot reliably read their newly written entry.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0043",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "Evidence: executed `finish_readable_zip64_preserves_sizes_and_contents`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0044",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "keep actual sizes in memory; apply sentinel values only during serialization.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0045",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "Empty XZ entries produce invalid compressed streams\n\n"
  },
  {
